From: ios-milanf-port (desk phase, 2026-10-25)
Subject: [PATCH] MILANF: the IOKit bring-up trace, which is the only instrument
 we have once userspace starts

0086-0095 gave the board a root filesystem, a CPU driver, working crypto, a
platform expert that does not assert, and an identity that does not claim to be
an iPad. None of that is observable on milanf, because milanf has no console
worth the name and no way to attach a debugger: what the next phase produces is
either a boot that reaches `launchd`, or silence after the last `IOLog`. The
instrument for that gap already exists in this tree - `sweetiolog`, added by
0049 and extended by 0054 - and it is compiled out on milanf by the same
narrow board gate as everything else.

The tracer answers one question, repeatedly, and it is the question the
userspace phase is really about: **which IOKit service did the process ask for
right before it gave up.** It hooks six registry entry points:

| site | function | what it reports |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2678 | `internal_io_service_get_matching_services` | matching dict -> count |
| 2778 | `is_io_service_get_matching_service` | matching dict -> class |
| 2888 | `is_io_service_get_matching_services_bin` | binary match -> class |
| 3363 | `is_io_registry_entry_from_path` | path -> class |
| 4181 | `is_io_registry_entry_get_child_iterator` | plane + class |
| 4404 | `IOUserClient::externalMethod` / open | open type -> return |

and it filters by process name against the boot-arg list, so it costs one
`proc_selfname` and a `strncmp` per call for every process it is not watching.
Default list is `backboardd,SpringBoard` (0054's value, unchanged here), `-`
disables it, and it self-limits at 400 lines per boot so a match storm cannot
bury the rest of the log.

Everything it calls is already ungated on milanf, which is why this is a
mechanical widening and not a port: `PE_parse_boot_argn` is pexpert
(`pexpert/pexpert/arm64/PE_parse_boot_argn.c`), `proc_selfname` is
`bsd/kern/kern_proc.c:1411`, `OSSafeReleaseNULL`/`OSSerialize` are libkern, and
`IOLog` is the console ring 0089 added. Nothing in the tracer reads a sweet
address, a sweet clock, or a sweet register - it walks the registry plane,
which both boards have.

One thing in here looks like a syntax error and is not, so it does not get
"fixed" by accident: `static ` on line 2602, with a trailing space and nothing
else on the line, followed by the `#if`. 0049 split what used to be

    static kern_return_t
    internal_io_service_get_matching_services(...)

so that the file-scope statics and helpers could be inserted between the
storage class and the declaration. Preprocessor-conditional text is invisible to
the grammar, so the enclosing guard is only ever *balanced* text: with the gate
open the reader sees `static <statics> kern_return_t <decl>` and with the gate
closed it sees `static kern_return_t <decl>`. Both are valid. Widening this
gate therefore changes nothing about the shape of the file - it only stops the
mixture from being board-specific.

The header comment is reworded board-neutrally for the same reason 0094's was:
it says "SWEET IOKit trace", and the statement stops being true of the gate once
both boards share it. The `sweetiolog` boot-arg name and the `"IOK %s:"` log
prefix are deliberately *not* touched - the host-side log tooling greps for
them, and on this port the strings are the interface.

Usage on milanf, which is why this patch exists at all:

    tools/mkpayload.py Image ADT out.img \
        --cmdline "debug=0xc serial=3 cpus=1 keepsyms=1 \
                   sweetiolog=launchd,backboardd,SpringBoard"

`launchd` is byte-identical in length to the default list's longest member and
the buffer is 128 bytes, so the list has room; it is specified on the command
line rather than in the default so that sweet's behaviour is untouched.

Verified without a compiler: fresh ci-art3, 0086-0096 applied one at a time in
order, every one exit 0, every touched file CR=0, patch CR=0. The gate count
assertion (`expected 7, found N`) is the load-bearing check here: all seven sites
are the same literal string, so a wrong base tree would otherwise produce a patch
that silently widens a different number of them. Link unverified; CI in the
morning.

